let's return to the rail strike story  now because there's widespread  disruption across the country with more  than 40 000 workers striking over pay  and conditions it's the second walkout  in three days and the sixth of the  summer with unions and the government  reportedly still some distance apart in  agreeing a settlement well joining me  live from london houston is the general  secretary of the rmt union mick lynch  thank you very much for joining us this  morning mr lynch could you just update  us on exactly where the negotiations are  at the moment please  well we'll be engaging with the  companies again next week starting on  monday both network rail and the train  operators  but what we've seen uh in the last  couple of days is a wave of hostility  emanating from grant shaps he's  threatened  uh yesterday to sack all these workers  behind me and workers across the country  by issuing statutory redundancy letters  he has also shown that he's completely  incompetent in regards to the railway he  doesn't know how it works  and he's making wild threats about  banning strikes and making trade unions  virtually illegal so he's escalating the  dispute  when where he should be is trying to  create an atmosphere of good will and  positivity so that we can work towards a  solution but at the root of it is his  is his decision to cut four billion  pounds both from  uh london underground at two billion and  the main line at two billion that's  caused the funding crisis in our  industry  and he's wanting our members to pay to  pay the price of that in terms of  maybe five or six thousand job cuts  a pay freeze that's gone on for two or  three years  uh ripping up our conditions issuing new  contracts of employment  and so therefore what he's now indulging  in is fire and re-hire  which he's taken the trouble to condemn  all through the summer he seems to  forgot forgotten that  he's also forgotten that about six or  eight weeks ago he said that he's going  to ban overtime on the railway and some  people have stopped working overtime and  therefore the railway's ground to a halt  in some areas so  it's a ridiculous and contradictory  situation where he wants to get rid of  thousands of workers  and at the same time demand that they  work overtime but on other occasions  demand that they don't  so he's got to get out of the way or  create an atmosphere of goodwill so that  i can negotiate along with my officials  a settlement to this to this uh  crisis and the the problems we've got  and get the railways back to running on  behalf of the people so that we can run  the system properly  and our passengers can benefit from the  services and the economy can benefit and  so can our environment  now a large part of this dispute focuses  on pay and the government say that the  pay offer that they've put forward would  they think be accepted by your members  but you don't want to put that pay deal  to your members why is that  well it shows how out of touch the  government are they've they've made a  payoff of a half the people in this  dispute the train operating companies  have made no offer  on pay and yesterday he said he was  going to make them all redundant  there is an offer on the table from  network rail which is fifty percent of  our members it's a three-year  pay deal at eight percent and and that  would have to cover next year as well as  this year and last year  inflation this week on the retail price  price index was 12.3  so if we accepted that pay offer we  would be getting about a quarter of next  year's inflation which is likely to be  15 or 16 percent so our members aren't  prepared to accept that but before we  even get on to pay we have to sort out  job security which we haven't done and  we have to work through the  modernization agenda that the companies  want and the changes the terms and  conditions and what they've essentially  said  across the railway is they want to rip  up our contracts make assign new  contracts and if we don't want to take  those  members are going to be put out work so  that's not an atmosphere when you make  an agreement i had a meeting on  wednesday night with 14 000 rmt members  attending it online and nobody put  forward the idea that we should accept  what's on the table we don't need a  referendum you can see on the streets of  london london and around the country  today a massive turnout on our picket  lines all of our members are rallying to  the call that this strike will go on  until we get a settlement we don't need  a referendum would be  could you explain to us what would be a  fair deal in your eyes  yes the basis of a fair deal is getting  a job security agreement so we don't get  thousands of people made redundant we  move on from there to work out the  conditions of service and the  modernization agenda we have to get  guarantees about the pension scheme  which they want to rip up they want to  dilute our benefits in retirement and  increase the contributions and then when  we've done that we can move on to a pay  package that has to address the cost of  living crisis  and the fact that we've not had a pay  rise for the last two or three years so  it's a package deal that we're trying to  negotiate and when we've addressed  all the subjects didn't that not just  pay which grant sap seems to be obsessed  with  seems to have forgotten the threats he's  made to our people  we can get to a solution that will be a  package deal that covers all the aspects  in the dispute  and i wanted to ask you looking at a  website read.com it says the average  train driver salary in the uk is 48 and  a half thousand pounds a year what do  you say to people who say  that the salaries are already high  enough and pay pay shouldn't be an issue  at this time when everybody is  struggling  well standing behind me now you can  there's not a train driver on this  picket line there are catering workers  there are train managers  there are station workers and all sorts  of people that are going to run the  railway their salaries are typically  about 25 to 30 000 pounds  a year they work around the clock  uh 363 days a year  running our railway in all weathers and  all times of the day at night they have  not had a pay rise for two or three  years most of them there are nothing  like 45 000 or any other figures that  grant chaps and new journalists quote  night and day they're on very modest pay  and they need a pay rise but of course  they won't get a pay rise unless they've  got a job so what we've got to do first  is secure their jobs and then move on to  the other aspects in the dispute which  is cutting up their terms and conditions  and a fire and rehire stance that's now  coming from the government when we've  solved that we can move on to the pay  but the pay is a long way from being  on the table now because we've got to  sort out all the other issues and if i  told my people behind me the figures  that you're quoting they would either  laugh or cry at what you're saying  because it's a complete distortion of  what railway workers have to put up with  and what they get paid  well we've got you i just wanted to ask  you about some comments you've made  about the ukraine situation and russia's  invasion i read that you said that the  eu has provoked trouble in ukraine and  it's wrong to purely blame russia for  the invasion do you stand by those  comments why do you believe that  i don't believe that and it's that's a  partial uh quote from a very long  conversation what i believe is it is no  justification for russia's  invasion of the ukraine there is nothing  that  that could warrant such an aggressive  and murderous act  the ukrainian people have the right to  live in the sovereign nation under their  own constitution making their own laws  in a democratic process  and what the russians need to do now is  cease their aggression  cease fire and withdraw  from all areas of the uh ukrainian  sovereign territory immediately and if  they do that  uh there can be some peace in that  region that what you've said is the  distortion of my beliefs russia has no  right to be in the ukraine and they  should get out straight away  did you say that the eu has provoked  trouble in ukraine  what i said was that there was a  quite a long run-up to this this this  issue but what i've also said on the  record numerous times  in public statements and written  statements that russia is completely  unjustified and their war of aggression  on the ukrainian people is an outrage  and it's probably illegal  and the leaders of the russian regime  including putin should be called to  account for  it lynch general secretary of the rmt  union thank you very much for joining us  this morning on sky news
 

